Create Date: ships with the template (include_metering)

``wallet_transactions`` is the only org-scoped metering table that carried no ``organization_id``:
it reached its tenant only through ``wallet_id -> customer_wallets``. That made it the one table
P4's RLS backstop could not protect on a local fact.

The column is DENORMALISED rather than reached through the parent, so the policy on this table is
the same single expression every other tenant table uses. The alternative — ``USING (EXISTS
(SELECT 1 FROM customer_wallets ...))`` — puts a per-row subquery on the highest-write table in the
schema and introduces a second policy shape that every future reader has to learn separately.

Expand→contract, so an existing service with rows can run it without a window where the column is
required but unset:

  1. add the column NULLable,
  2. backfill from the parent wallet,
  3. only then set NOT NULL and index it.

Step 2 is the whole reason this is not a one-liner: an already-deployed service has wallet rows, and
`0013_rls_backfill` will refuse to protect a table whose tenant column is null.
